        \"title\": <report_title>,\n        \"summary\": <executive_summary>,\n        \"rating\": <impact_severity_rating>,\n        \"rating_explanation\": <rating_explanation>,\n        \"findings\": [\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_1_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_1_explanation>\n            },\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_2_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_2_explanation>\n            }\n        ]\n    }\n\n# Grounding Rules\n\nPoints supported by data should list their data references as follows:\n\n\"This is an example sentence supported by multiple data references [Data: <dataset name> (record ids); <dataset name> (record ids)].\"\n\nDo not list more than 5 record ids in a single reference. Instead, list the top 5 most relevant record ids and add \"+more\" to indicate that there are more.\n\nFor example:\n\"Person X is the owner of Company Y and subject to many allegations of wrongdoing [Data: Reports (1), Entities (5, 7); Relationships (23); Claims (7, 2, 34, 64, 46, +more)].\"\n\nwhere 1, 5, 7, 23, 2, 34, 46, and 64 represent the id (not the index) of the relevant data record.\n\nDo not include information where the supporting evidence for it is not provided.\n\nLimit the total report length to 2500 words.\n\n# Example Input\n-----------\nText:\n\nEntities\n\nid,entity,description\n5,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n6,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is an organization that is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n\nRelationships\n\nid,source,target,description\n37,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n38,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n39,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,The Unity March is taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n40,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,TRIBUNE SPOTLIGHT,Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity march taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n41,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,BAILEY ASADI,Bailey Asadi is speaking at Verdant Oasis Plaza about the march\n43,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,UNITY MARCH,Harmony Assembly is organizing the Unity March\n\nOutput:\n{\n    \"title\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza and Unity March\",\n    \"summary\": \"The community revolves around the Verdant Oasis Plaza, which is the location of the Unity March. The plaza has relationships with the Harmony Assembly, Unity March, and Tribune Spotlight, all of which are associated with the march event.\",\n    \"rating\": 5.0,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is moderate due to the potential for unrest or conflict during the Unity March.\",\n    \"findings\": [\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza as the central location\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza is the central entity in this community, serving as the location for the Unity March. This plaza is the common link between all other entities, suggesting its significance in the community. The plaza's association with the march could potentially lead to issues such as public disorder or conflict, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. [Data: Entities (5), Relationships (37, 38, 39, 40, 41,+more)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Harmony Assembly's role in the community\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Harmony Assembly is another key entity in this community, being the organizer of the march at Verdant Oasis Plaza. The nature of Harmony Assembly and its march could be a potential source of threat, depending on their objectives and the reactions they provoke. The relationship between Harmony Assembly and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Entities(6), Relationships (38, 43)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Unity March as a significant event\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The Unity March is a significant event taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza. This event is a key factor in the community's dynamics and could be a potential source of threat, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. The relationship between the march and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Relationships (39)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Role of Tribune Spotlight\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity March taking place in Verdant Oasis Plaza. This suggests that the event has attracted media attention, which could amplify its impact on the community. The role of Tribune Spotlight could be significant in shaping public perception of the event and the entities involved. [Data: Relationships (40)]\"\n        }\n    ]\n}\n\n\n# Real Data\n\nUse the following text for your answer. Do not make anything up in your answer.\n\nText:\n-----Entities-----\nhuman_readable_id,title,description,degree\r\n9,CONTRAST,\"**Entity: CONTRAST**\n\nA contrast is a statistical tool used in the analysis of variance (ANOVA) and other statistical methods to test specific hypotheses regarding group means. It is defined as a linear combination of group means or parameters, allowing researchers to make comparisons between different treatment levels or groups within an experimental design. The primary purpose of a contrast is to evaluate specific hypotheses about the differences among group means, facilitating a more nuanced understanding of the effects of various factors in an experiment.\n\nMathematically, a contrast can be expressed as:\n\n\\[\nC = \\sum_{i=1}^{k} c_i \\mu_i\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(C\\) is the contrast, \\(c_i\\) are the coefficients that define the contrast, and \\(\\mu_i\\) are the group means. The coefficients \\(c_i\\) must sum to zero (\\(\\sum_{i=1}^{k} c_i = 0\\)) to ensure that the contrast is centered around the overall mean, allowing for meaningful comparisons.\n\nIn terms of design layout, contrasts are particularly useful in factorial designs, where multiple treatment combinations are tested. They enable researchers to focus on specific comparisons, such as differences between levels of a factor or interactions between factors, rather than conducting multiple pairwise comparisons.\n\nThe validity of contrasts relies on several assumptions, including the normality of the residuals, homogeneity of variances (homoscedasticity), and independence of observations. These assumptions are critical for ensuring that the results of the contrast are reliable and interpretable.\n\nContrasts are applicable in various scenarios, particularly when researchers wish to test specific hypotheses rather than conducting a general analysis of variance. They are selected based on the research questions posed and the structure of the experimental design. For instance, contrasts are often used in planned comparisons, where specific hypotheses are formulated prior to data collection.\n\nThe analysis procedure for contrasts typically involves calculating the contrast value and its associated standard error, followed by hypothesis testing using t-tests or F-tests, depending on the context. The significance of the contrast can be assessed to determine whether the differences among group means are statistically significant.\n\nAdvantages of using contrasts include their ability to provide focused comparisons, reduce the risk of Type I errors associated with multiple comparisons, and enhance the interpretability of results. They allow researchers to test specific hypotheses that are of interest, making them a powerful tool in experimental analysis.\n\nHowever, contrasts also have limitations. They require careful selection of coefficients, and improper specification can lead to misleading conclusions. Additionally, the assumptions underlying contrasts must be met for the results to be valid, which may not always be the case in practice.\n\nRelated designs and methods include planned comparisons, post-hoc tests, and other forms of hypothesis testing in the context of ANOVA. Alternatives to contrasts may involve using multiple comparison procedures, such as Tukey's HSD or Bonferroni corrections, which provide a broader analysis of group differences.\n\nPractical implementation considerations for contrasts include ensuring that the experimental design is appropriately structured to allow for the desired comparisons and that the assumptions of the statistical tests are verified. Researchers should also be mindful of the interpretation of results, particularly in the context of the specific hypotheses being tested.\n\nIn terms of software implementation, many statistical software packages, such as R, SAS, and SPSS, provide built-in functions for conducting contrast analyses, making it accessible for researchers to apply these methods in their studies.\n\nCommon applications of contrasts can be found in various fields, including agriculture, psychology, medicine, and social sciences, where researchers seek to understand the effects of different treatments or interventions on specific outcomes. Examples include comparing the effectiveness of different drug dosages, evaluating educational interventions, or assessing the impact of various agricultural practices on crop yield.\",3\r\n8,LINEAR FUNCTION,\"### Comprehensive Summary of Linear Function\n\n**Definition and Purpose**  \nA linear function is a mathematical function that can be graphically represented as a straight line on a Cartesian plane. It is defined by a linear equation of the form \\( y = mx + b \\), where \\( m \\) represents the slope of the line, \\( b \\) is the y-intercept, and \\( x \\) is the independent variable. In the context of random variables, a linear function represents a linear combination of those variables, allowing for the modeling of relationships between them.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation**  \nThe general form of a linear function can be expressed as:\n\\[\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2 + \\ldots + \\beta_k x_k + \\epsilon\n\\]\nwhere \\( \\beta_0 \\) is the intercept, \\( \\beta_1, \\beta_2, \\ldots, \\beta_k \\) are the coefficients representing the effect of each independent variable \\( x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_k \\), and \\( \\epsilon \\) is the error term.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure**  \nIn experimental designs, linear functions can be utilized to model the relationship between independent variables (factors) and a dependent variable (response). The design layout may involve various configurations such as completely randomized designs, factorial designs, or regression designs, depending on the number of factors and levels involved.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**  \nThe validity of linear functions, particularly in the context of regression analysis, relies on several key assumptions:\n1. Linearity: The relationship between the independent and dependent variables is linear.\n2. Independence: The residuals (errors) are independent.\n3. Homoscedasticity: The residuals have constant variance across all levels of the independent variables.\n4. Normality: The residuals are normally distributed.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**  \nLinear functions are applicable when the relationship between the variables is expected to be linear. They are particularly useful in situations where the response variable is continuous, and the independent variables can be either continuous or categorical. Selection criteria include the nature of the data, the underlying theoretical framework, and the need for interpretability.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nThe analysis of linear functions typically involves fitting the model to the data using methods such as Ordinary Least Squares (OLS). The goodness of fit can be assessed using R-squared values, residual plots, and hypothesis tests for the coefficients.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \nLinear functions are straightforward to interpret and computationally efficient. They provide a clear framework for understanding relationships between variables and can be easily extended to multiple regression scenarios. Additionally, they serve as a foundation for more complex modeling techniques.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \nThe primary limitation of linear functions is their assumption of linearity, which may not hold true in all cases. They can also be sensitive to outliers, which can disproportionately influence the results. Furthermore, linear models may oversimplify complex relationships, leading to inadequate predictions.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nRelated methods include polynomial regression for modeling non-linear relationships, generalized linear models (GLMs) for accommodating different types of response variables, and non-parametric methods when assumptions of linearity are violated.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nWhen implementing linear functions, it is crucial to conduct exploratory data analysis to check for linearity, independence, and homoscedasticity. Model diagnostics should be performed to validate the assumptions and ensure the robustness of the model.\n\n**Software Implementation**  \nLinear functions can be implemented using various statistical software packages, including R, Python (with libraries such as statsmodels and scikit-learn), SAS, and SPSS. These tools provide functions for fitting linear models, conducting hypothesis tests, and generating diagnostic plots.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**  \nLinear functions are widely used in fields such as economics, biology, engineering, and social sciences. Common applications include predicting sales based on advertising spend, modeling the relationship between temperature and energy consumption, and analyzing the impact of educational interventions on student performance.\",3\r\n37,RANDOM VARIABLE,\"### Comprehensive Summary of Random Variable\n\nA **random variable** is a fundamental concept in probability and statistics, defined as a variable whose possible values are numerical outcomes resulting from a random phenomenon. It serves as a bridge between the theoretical framework of probability and the practical application of statistical analysis, particularly in the context of experimental designs and data analysis.\n\n#### Definition and Purpose\nThe primary purpose of a random variable is to quantify the outcomes of random processes, allowing researchers to model uncertainty and variability in data. In experimental contexts, such as measuring bond strength, random variables help in understanding how chance influences the results, enabling the analysis of variability and the drawing of inferences from experimental data.\n\n#### Mathematical Formulation\nMathematically, a random variable \\(X\\) can be classified into two types:\n1. **Discrete Random Variable**: Takes on a countable number of distinct values. For example, the number of successes in a series of trials.\n2. **Continuous Random Variable**: Takes on an infinite number of possible values within a given range. For example, the measurement of bond strength can be modeled as a continuous random variable.\n\nThe probability distribution function (PDF) or probability mass function (PMF) describes the likelihood of each outcome. For a discrete random variable, the PMF is defined as:\n\\[\nP(X = x) = p(x)\n\\]\nFor a continuous random variable, the PDF is defined such that:\n\\[\nP(a < X < b) = \\int_{a}^{b} f(x) \\, dx\n\\]\nwhere \\(f(x)\\) is the PDF.\n\n#### Design Layout\nIn the context of experimental designs, random variables are often used to represent the response variables that are influenced by various factors. The layout of an experiment may involve randomization to ensure that the effects of uncontrolled variables are minimized, thereby allowing for a clearer interpretation of the effects of the factors being studied.\n\n#### Assumptions Required for Validity\nThe validity of analyses involving random variables relies on several assumptions:\n- Independence: The values of the random variable should be independent of each other.\n- Identically Distributed: The random variable should follow the same probability distribution across observations.\n- Finite Variance: The variance of the random variable should be finite.\n\n#### When to Use / Applicability\nRandom variables are applicable in any scenario where outcomes are uncertain and can be quantified. They are particularly useful in experimental designs where the goal is to assess the impact of various factors on a response variable, such as in the analysis of bond strength in materials testing.\n\n#### Analysis Procedure\nThe analysis of random variables often involves statistical methods such as hypothesis testing, confidence intervals, and regression analysis. In the context of ANOVA (Analysis of Variance), random variables are used to assess the differences between group means and to determine if the observed variations are statistically significant.\n\n#### Advantages and Strengths\n- Provides a structured way to quantify uncertainty and variability.\n- Facilitates the application of statistical methods to analyze experimental data.\n- Enables the modeling of complex phenomena through probability distributions.\n\n#### Limitations and Disadvantages\n- Requires a clear understanding of probability theory, which may be challenging for some practitioners.\n- The assumptions of independence and identical distribution may not always hold in real-world scenarios, potentially leading to biased results.\n\n#### Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives\nRandom variables are integral to various statistical methods, including:\n- ANOVA\n- Regression Analysis\n- Bayesian Statistics\n- Monte Carlo Simulations\n\n#### Practical Implementation Considerations\nWhen implementing random variables in experimental designs, researchers should ensure proper randomization techniques are employed to mitigate bias. Additionally, the choice of probability distribution should be carefully considered based on the nature of the data.\n\n#### Software Implementation\nStatistical software packages such as R, Python (with libraries like NumPy and SciPy), SAS, and SPSS provide tools for modeling random variables and conducting analyses involving them.\n\n#### Common Applications or Examples\nRandom variables are widely used in fields such as:\n- Quality control (e.g., measuring defects in manufacturing)\n- Clinical trials (e.g., measuring patient responses to treatment)\n- Environmental studies (e.g., assessing pollutant levels)\n\nIn summary, random variables are essential components in the design and analysis of experiments, providing a framework for understanding and quantifying the effects of chance on observed outcomes.\",3\r\n151,PARAMETRIC FUNCTION,\"### Comprehensive Summary of Parametric Function\n\n**Definition and Purpose**  \nA parametric function is a mathematical function that defines a relationship between variables through the use of parameters. These parameters can be estimated using statistical methods, allowing for the modeling of complex relationships in data. The primary purpose of a parametric function is to provide a structured way to represent and analyze the relationship between independent and dependent variables in various fields, including statistics, engineering, and economics.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure**  \nThe general form of a parametric function can be expressed as \\( y = f(x; \\theta) \\), where \\( y \\) is the dependent variable, \\( x \\) represents the independent variable(s), and \\( \\theta \\) denotes the parameters of the function. The specific form of \\( f \\) can vary widely, including linear models (e.g., \\( y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x \\)), polynomial models, exponential models, and more complex forms depending on the nature of the data and the underlying relationship being modeled.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**  \nThe validity of parametric functions relies on several assumptions, including:\n- The relationship between the variables is correctly specified (i.e., the chosen functional form accurately reflects the underlying relationship).\n- The parameters are estimated from a representative sample of data.\n- The residuals (differences between observed and predicted values) are normally distributed and homoscedastic (constant variance).\n- Independence of observations.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**  \nParametric functions are applicable when:\n- The relationship between the variables is expected to follow a specific functional form.\n- Sufficient data is available to estimate the parameters reliably.\n- The assumptions of normality and homoscedasticity can be reasonably met.\nSelection criteria include the nature of the data, the underlying theoretical framework, and the complexity of the relationship being modeled.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nThe analysis of parametric functions typically involves:\n1. Specifying the functional form of the model.\n2. Estimating the parameters using methods such as Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) for linear models or Maximum Likelihood Estimation (MLE) for more complex models.\n3. Evaluating the goodness-of-fit using statistical tests (e.g., R-squared, F-tests) and diagnostic plots.\n4. Conducting hypothesis tests on the parameters to assess their significance.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \n- Parametric functions can provide a clear and interpretable model of relationships between variables.\n- They often require fewer data points to estimate parameters compared to non-parametric methods.\n- They allow for the incorporation of prior knowledge about the relationship through the choice of functional form.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \n- The primary limitation of parametric functions is their reliance on the correct specification of the functional form; incorrect specifications can lead to biased estimates and misleading conclusions.\n- They may not perform well with small sample sizes or when the assumptions of normality and homoscedasticity are violated.\n- They can be less flexible than non-parametric methods, which do not assume a specific functional form.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nRelated methods include non-parametric functions, which do not assume a specific form and can be more flexible in modeling relationships. Examples include kernel smoothing and spline regression. Additionally, generalized linear models (GLMs) extend parametric functions to accommodate non-normal response distributions.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nWhen implementing parametric functions, practitioners should ensure that the assumptions are checked and validated through diagnostic plots and statistical tests. It is also important to consider the potential for overfitting, especially in complex models with many parameters.\n\n**Software Implementation**  \nParametric functions can be implemented in various statistical software packages, including R, Python (using libraries such as statsmodels and scikit-learn), SAS, and SPSS. These tools provide functions for model fitting, parameter estimation, and diagnostic checking.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**  \nParametric functions are widely used in fields such as economics (e.g., modeling demand and supply), biology (e.g., growth models), and engineering (e.g., stress-strain relationships). A common example is the linear regression model, which is a specific type of parametric function used to predict outcomes based on one or more predictor variables.\",2\r\n\n\n-----Relationships-----\nhuman_readable_id,source,target,description,combined_degree\r\n1847,CONTRAST,TREATMENT COMBINATIONS,Contrast is used to evaluate the effects of different treatment combinations in factorial designs.,25\r\n6,LINEAR FUNCTION,CONTRAST,A contrast is a specific type of linear fun